1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:07,373
Scope and Settle helps freelance designers turn a client request into
a clear agreement and a paid change.

2
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:15,172
Here is the actual Figma comment, alongside the selected desktop
checkout frame.

3
00:00:15,172 --> 00:00:17,146
The design supplies context.

4
00:00:17,146 --> 00:00:20,600
The agreed brief determines what is included.

5
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:26,058
This synthetic brief explicitly excludes mobile layouts from the
original fee.

6
00:00:26,058 --> 00:00:33,052
It also requires a separate scope and price, approved by both the
designer and the client.

7
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:41,304
This is the real DeepSeek assessment returned through Baseten, with
the selected frame attached.

8
00:00:41,304 --> 00:00:48,655
It classifies the request as new work, cites the two agreement
clauses, and describes the desktop design.

9
00:00:48,655 --> 00:00:52,546
The model can also return included or needs clarification.

10
00:00:52,546 --> 00:00:58,600
It suggests a rate-card item; the designer remains responsible for the
deliverables and price.

11
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:06,450
The designer reviews the mobile deliverable and confirms the two
hundred and fifty dollar rate-card price.

12
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:10,950
The client gets a separate page for that exact revision.

13
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:15,900
Approval requires explicit consent to the deliverables, total, and
payment-before-work term.

14
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:18,600
The approval transition is condensed here.

15
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:24,303
Only after approval does the backend create the PayPal sandbox
invoice.

16
00:01:24,303 --> 00:01:26,600
Approval itself is not payment.

17
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:31,702
This is the actual PayPal invoice for the same change.

18
00:01:31,702 --> 00:01:36,600
If preparation is interrupted, recovery reconciles and reuses the
existing invoice.

19
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:41,706
The dedicated sandbox buyer completes the purchase in PayPal.

20
00:01:41,706 --> 00:01:44,677
Authentication is omitted from this recording.

21
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:49,134
This is a real sandbox transaction using test funds.

22
00:01:49,134 --> 00:01:52,600
Payment is reconciled against PayPal’s own invoice.

23
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:58,067
PayPal invoice retrieval confirms Paid on the same revision.

24
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:01,278
Its signed callback was separately verified.

25
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:08,730
Back in the released Figma plugin, Check payment confirms Paid and
zero due.

26
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:13,788
The approval and payment remain attached to revision one of this
change.

27
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:20,522
Close and reopen the plugin, and the same paid revision returns.

28
00:02:20,522 --> 00:02:26,273
Work begins after payment is a commercial term; Figma editing remains
available.

29
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:35,407
Try the hosted judge entry, released plugin, or public MIT source with
local setup.

30
00:02:35,407 --> 00:02:38,925
This prototype supports one synthetic Figma project, U.S.

31
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:40,684
dollars, and sandbox accounts.

32
00:02:40,684 --> 00:02:42,443
Designer walkthroughs are pending.

33
00:02:42,443 --> 00:02:48,600
The goal is a traceable path from a request to an agreed, paid change.
